<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Botswana Democratic Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1962">1962</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bechuanaland Democratic Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> was founded as the Bechuanaland Democratic Party in 1962 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a> and others. It won the pre-independence election of 1965 and governed Botswana without interruption from independence in 1966, under Seretse Khama and his successors, until its defeat in the general election of 2024.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/botswana-democratic-party.jpg" alt="Botswana Democratic Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mangwanani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Botswana_Democratic_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was the third founded in the British protectorate of Bechuanaland, where political life developed slowly and peacefully. It was set up at Lobatse by members of the African Consultative Council under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a>, former head of the BaNgwato, with Ketumile Quett Masire as general secretary, and held its first constitutive congress on 28 February 1962. Masire began the party newspaper Therisanyo in 1963. On 30 September 1966, the day Bechuanaland became the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, the party took its present name, with Khama as president and Masire as vice-president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Khama did not seek a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and kept a responsible opposition in Parliament; his government also kept foreign expatriates in the civil service until local replacements were qualified. The party's dominance in the following decades owed much to an opposition divided by tribal interests or by African socialism, and to its own hold on the Central District, the Ngwato homeland. Khama held the presidency until his death in 1980.</p><p>Between 1989 and 1993 growth of more than 13% gave way to the first recession since independence, and urbanisation and land scandals eroded support: in 1994 the party won only 26 of 39 seats. Under Ian Khama, president from 1 April 2008, factional quarrels led to the Botswana Movement for Democracy splitting off in 2010, though the party took 45 of 57 seats in 2009. After Mokgweetsi Masisi succeeded in 2018, the Khama family left and the Botswana Patriotic Front took much of the Central District, while urban gains kept the party's majority in 2019.</p><p>In the 2024 general election the party won about 30% of the vote but, its support spread thinly under first-past-the-post voting, kept only four of 61 seats. Masisi conceded two days later, and in a peaceful transfer of power Boko of the opposition UDC was sworn in as president, forming the first government since independence without the party. At its 41st National Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maun">Maun</a> the party elected Mpho Balopi as leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Analysts describe the party's ideology as broad, conservative and centre-right, with national unity, economic development and political democracy as its official core values, beside the ideas of kagisano, togetherness, and botho, humanity. Its early governments pursued free-market policies and low taxes to attract mining investment, spending the revenue on infrastructure, health and education.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Botswana Democratic Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seretse Khama returned to lead the <b>Bechuanaland Democratic Party</b>, which won the 1965 election.</blockquote><a href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bechuanaland Democratic Party</b>, founded by Seretse Khama in 1962, won the first general election.</blockquote><a href="/state/botswana">Botswana</a></li></ul></section>
